  "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250054/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$",
